# STATUS — @PROJECT@

**Scaffolded @DATE@.** This document is the project's measured state. The rule
it exists to enforce: **every number here names the machine and the date it was
measured on, or it does not belong here.** Nothing is quoted from another
session, another title, or another person's run.

Right now almost nothing has been measured. That is the correct content for a
freshly cut project, and it is more useful than a template full of placeholders
that read like results.

---

## 1. ROM identity — [MEASURED] @DATE@

Read off the dump by `n64lle tools/new_project/probe_rom.py`. The CIC is the one
field that tool derives rather than reads — it re-implements the IPL2 checksum in
Python, because scaffolding necessarily runs before the framework is built. If
you scaffolded with `--generate`, the scaffolder already cross-checked it against
n64lle's own detector and would have refused to continue on a mismatch. If you
did not, run the check yourself before trusting the row below, and say here that
you did:

```sh
N64_RSP_EXEC=1 ./build/@SLUG@-frame-probe @ROM_FILE@ /dev/null 1 1 | head -1
```

| | |
|---|---|
| name | @NAME@ |
| cart id | `@CARTID@` |
| region | `@REGION@` (@REGION_LABEL@) |
| revision | @REVISION@ |
| entry PC | `@ENTRY_PC@` |
| size | @ROM_SIZE@ bytes (@ROM_SIZE_H@) |
| byte order | `@BYTE_ORDER@` |
| CIC | `@CIC@`, seed `@CIC_SEED@`, challenge `@CIC_CHALLENGE@` |
| CRC1 / CRC2 | `@CRC1@` / `@CRC2@` |
| SHA-256 | `@SHA256@` |

@CIC_NOTE@

## 2. The pipeline — not yet recorded

Fill this in from your own run. The shape to record:

| Stage | Result | Wall |
|---|---|---|
| `n64lle-harvest`, @HARVEST_FRAMES@-frame window, `N64_RSP_EXEC=1` | executed instructions, run-generations, MMIO traps | |
| `n64emit --image` | programs emitted / skipped, lines of C | |
| compile | | |
| `ctest` | of 7 | |

`N64_RSP_EXEC=1` on the harvest is load-bearing: without it no graphics task
ever runs and discovery under-covers by tens of thousands of instructions while
still looking like a clean result. If your two harvests disagree, check the flag
before you check the game.

## 3. Speed — not yet recorded

Timing claims come from `@SLUG@-bench`, the paired in-process tier A/B, never
from a single-shot run and never as a cross-session absolute. State whether the
RSP and the rasterizer were live: `bench_driver` does not enable them, so its
ratio is CPU-only and is **not** an end-to-end figure.

## 4. Rendering — not yet observed

`@SLUG@-frame-probe` is the tool, and the family rule is to screenshot before
asserting anything about visible state. Ring counters and command totals are
instrument state, not pixels.

What a frame from the probe or the host proves: correctness **against the
interpreter**. Not against hardware — the Ares oracle differentials do not build
off Windows, so nothing in this repo is graded against real silicon yet.

## 5. Dispatch misses — UNTRIAGED

Every host run prints `DISPATCH_STATS` and says so out loud when it produced
misses. Project law puts resolving them ahead of all other debugging, and the
triage is what populates `game.toml [audit].regions`. Record the count, the
distinct-leader count, and what each leader turned out to be.

## 6. Open items, ordered

1. **Run the pipeline and triage the dispatch misses.** Before any other
   debugging, and before trusting any performance number.
2. **Establish this cart's save device.** n64lle models none — `si.c`: "We model
   no EEPROM and no RTC", and `pi.c`'s save database carries no EEPROM rows at
   all. `game.toml` says `save_type = "unknown"` rather than guessing, and the
   launcher hides its SAVES panel entirely. Establishing the real device is a
   measurement, not an inference from the cart's size or year.
3. **Decide the distribution posture** — it gates `.gitignore`. n64lle
   `docs/05-PROPOSAL.md` §10 says per-game repos hold *committed* `generated/`;
   `docs/DISTRIBUTION-POSTURE.md` recommends Posture A (nothing ROM-derived on
   project hosting) and reserves the call for the owner. `generated/` stays
   gitignored until then: the reversible choice.
4. **Choose a licence and a remote.** Both are decisions, not defaults.
5. **Owner only — play it with a real controller.** Whether the game responds
   the way a player expects is a gameplay verdict. Per the workspace standing
   ruling it is not an assistant's to give from instrument state.
